// src/v4/std/logic.dag
//
// Scope: classical two-valued logic — the `Bool` truth value and its
//        algebraic grounding. The boolean kernel every predicate returns.
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boolean_algebra
//         (two-element / classical bivalent logic; the Boolean data type)
//
// Owns:
//   - Bool — the closed two-variant truth value (a `Disj` of True | False)
//   - inhabitance: Bool inhabits `BooleanAlgebra` (std/algebra.dag) —
//     conjunction / disjunction / negation FALL OUT of the algebra; they
//     are never re-declared here (epistemic stacking)
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Disj
//   - std/algebra.dag: BooleanAlgebra
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-3 (scalar/numeric stack). Replaces
//   part of the deleted std/primitive.dag; see src/v4/STRUCTURE.md
//   §"scalar/numeric concept decomposition".
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.std.logic

import v4.std.algebra { BooleanAlgebra, BoundedLattice, Lattice }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// SUBSTRATE — node.dag + algebra.dag only. `Bool` is a `Disj`: the
// `type X = A | B` surface form (the same form as algebra.dag's
// `Ordering`), so the `Disj` connective needs no import. `BooleanAlgebra`
// / `BoundedLattice` / `Lattice` ARE imported from std/algebra.dag —
// they are referenced by name to type and construct the inhabitance
// instance below.
//
// INHABITANCE IS AN INSTANCE VALUE, NOT A CARRIER (operator-ratified).
// `Bool`'s grounding in Boolean algebra is the `data bool_boolean_algebra`
// value below — a concrete `BooleanAlgebra<Bool>`. There is no `inhabits`
// keyword and no inhabitance carrier type; the grounding simply IS this
// `data` instance. Conjunction / disjunction / negation are the
// instance's `meet` / `join` / `complement` fields — they are NOT
// re-declared as standalone named functions (epistemic stacking: the
// algebra is the definition). A consumer negates a bool by walking
// `bool_boolean_algebra.complement`.
//
// LAWS ARE GROUNDED DEFINITIONS, NEVER FLAGS (feedback_no_annotations).
// The Boolean-algebra axioms — absorption, complementation
// (a ∧ ¬a = ⊥, a ∨ ¬a = ⊤), distributivity — are the cited definition of
// `BooleanAlgebra` (Huntington 1904); the truth tables in the instance
// are the grounded definitions of the three operations. Neither is a
// stored flag or a hand-built equation AST.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Bool — the classical two-valued truth value: the boolean kernel every
// predicate returns. A `Disj` of exactly two nullary variants.
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boolean_algebra (classical
// bivalent logic — the two-element Boolean algebra).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: a truth value is consumed uniformly —
//      every predicate result and every `Branch` condition dispatches on
//      it; it is not a scattered per-consumer fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: promoting to `{ value: Int }` (1 / 0)
//      replaces a closed 2-valued exhaustive set with an unbounded Int
//      (`value = 42` representable) — violates P2 + the closed-set rule
//      (a closed outcome set is an enum, never a numeric proxy).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: `Bool` is the CARRIER `BooleanAlgebra` is
//      imposed on (`bool_boolean_algebra` below), not a type derived
//      FROM a richer std/ structure. The algebra grounds the operations;
//      the two elements themselves are irreducible — a carrier is a root,
//      the same status as algebra.dag's `FreeMonoid`.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a truth value is EXACTLY one of True / False
//      — mutually exclusive by the law of non-contradiction, never
//      simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: True / False are not a mechanical
//      `F<X>` shape repeated over a separately-declared set; they are two
//      irreducible nullary constants. No generic / projection (Practice
//      7) collapses them.
// Terminal: the irreducible classical bivalent truth values — the
// two-element Boolean algebra, mathematical consensus (Boole 1847).
type Bool
  = True
  | False


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// bool_boolean_algebra — Bool's inhabitance of `BooleanAlgebra` (the
// structure declared in T-2's std/algebra.dag). An INSTANCE VALUE: the
// grounding IS this `data`, never a carrier or an `inhabits` relation.
//
// The operations fall out of the instance:
//   - `meet`       — conjunction (∧): True ∧ b = b,  False ∧ b = False.
//   - `join`       — disjunction (∨): True ∨ b = True, False ∨ b = b.
//   - `complement` — negation (¬):    ¬True = False,  ¬False = True.
//   - `top`        — True:  the meet-identity / join-absorbing element.
//   - `bottom`     — False: the join-identity / meet-absorbing element.
// The `match` truth tables are the grounded definitions; the absorption /
// complement / distributivity axioms are `BooleanAlgebra`'s own cited
// definition (Huntington 1904), satisfied by these tables.
data bool_boolean_algebra: BooleanAlgebra<Bool> = BooleanAlgebra {
  bounded_lattice: BoundedLattice {
    lattice: Lattice {
      meet: fn(a, b) {
        match a {
          True => b
          False => False
        }
      }
      join: fn(a, b) {
        match a {
          True => True
          False => b
        }
      }
    }
    top: True
    bottom: False
  }
  complement: fn(a) {
    match a {
      True => False
      False => True
    }
  }
}
